[Chapter "Queen's Gambit"]
[Group "1...d5: the Queen's Gambit"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit"]
[ECO "D06"]
[Diagram "2.c4"]
[Course "queens-gambit"]
[Page "qg"]

{White offers the c-pawn to lure Black's d-pawn away from the center. Black declines it with 2...e6 or 2...c6, or accepts it with 2...dxc4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 {This is the Queen's Gambit.} *

[Chapter "Indian Defenses"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian Defenses"]
[Opening "Indian Defense: Normal Variation"]
[ECO "A50"]
[Diagram "2.c4"]
[Page "indian"]

{Black develops the knight first and keeps the choice of setup open: the King's Indian, Nimzo-Indian, Queen's Indian, Grünfeld or Benoni.}
1. d4 Nf6 {Black stops e4 and waits to choose a setup.} 2. c4 {The most common reply. White takes more of the center.} *

[Chapter "Dutch Defense"]
[Group "Other replies"]
[Opening "Dutch Defense"]
[ECO "A80"]
[Diagram "4...Be7"]

{Black claims e4 with the f-pawn, for unbalanced play and kingside chances, at some cost to king safety.

White: Fight for e4 with Nc3 and Bg5, which pins a defender of e4. Aim at the light squares that ...f5 loosened, such as e6 and the e8–h5 diagonal.

Black: Claim e4 with ...f5 and ...Nf6, then pick a setup: Classical (...e6, ...Be7, ...d6), Stonewall (...e6, ...d5) or Leningrad (...g6). Castle, then attack on the kingside with ...Qe8–h5 or ...f4. Keep an eye on your own king, which ...f5 weakened.}
1. d4 f5 {This is the Dutch Defense. The f-pawn controls e4.}
2. c4 Nf6 3. Nc3 {Fights for e4.} e6 {The Classical setup: ...Be7 and castling follow.}
4. Bg5 {Pins the knight that guards e4.} Be7 {Breaks the pin.} *

[Chapter "Budapest Gambit"]
[Group "Other replies"]
[Opening "Indian Defense: Budapest Gambit"]
[ECO "A51"]
[Diagram "4...Nc6"]

{Black offers the e-pawn at once and usually wins it back with ...Ng4 and ...Nc6. Best as a surprise.

White: Hold e5 for a while with Nf3 and Bf4, but keep developing; giving the pawn back calmly is fine. Watch the trap 5.Bf4 Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 Qe7 7.a3 Ngxe5 8.axb4?? Nd3#.

Black: Win back e5 with ...Nc6, ...Bb4+ and ...Qe7, developing quickly as you go. Stockfish rates the gambit about half a pawn worse for Black, so it works best as a surprise.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e5 {This is the Budapest Gambit. Black offers the e-pawn at once.}
3. dxe5 Ng4 {The knight hits e5 straight away.}
4. Nf3 {The Adler Variation. The knight guards e5.} Nc6 {Attacks e5 a second time.} *

[Chapter "London System"]
[Group "White's systems"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Diagram "4...c5"]

{White plays the same setup almost every game: Bf4, e3, Nf3, c3 and Nbd2. Easy to learn and hard to break.

White: Play the same setup almost every game: d4, Bf4, e3, Nf3, c3, Nbd2, Bd3 and castling. The c3–d4–e3 pawn triangle is hard to break. Later, Ne5 and a kingside attack are typical.

Black: Hit d4 with ...c5 and ...Nc6, and use ...Qb6 against the b2-pawn the f4 bishop left behind. Trade off White's good bishop with ...Bd6, or chase it with ...Nh5.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 {This is the London System. The bishop comes out before e3 can shut it in.} Nf6
3. e3 {Builds the pawn triangle.} e6
4. Nf3 c5 {The most active answer: Black hits d4 at once.} *

[Chapter "Colle System"]
[Group "White's systems"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Colle System"]
[ECO "D04"]
[Diagram "4...c5"]

{White builds d4, e3, Bd3 and Nbd2, then breaks with e4. Quiet and easy to learn.

White: Build the same setup every game: d4, Nf3, e3, Bd3, Nbd2, c3 and castling. Then break with e3–e4, which opens lines toward Black's king for the d3 bishop.

Black: Develop with ...c5, ...Nc6, ...Bd6 and castling, and meet e4 by trading in the center. Bringing the c8 bishop out to f5 or g4 before ...e6 (the Anti-Colle) blunts White's d3 bishop.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. e3 {This is the Colle System. White builds a solid center first.} e6 {Black's most common reply. It shuts in the c8 bishop, so 3...Bf5 and 3...Bg4 are popular too.}
4. Bd3 {The bishop aims at h7 and supports e4.} c5 {Black hits d4 at once.} *
